      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: Security & Secrets Management -->
    <section class="mb-12 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">06.</span> Security Boundaries: `secrets: inherit` vs Explicit Input Passing
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        The fundamental security differentiator between the two paradigms lies in secret propagation and OIDC isolation. In Reusable Workflows, the parent workflow can restrict access to sensitive production secrets using explicit secret contracts:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-cyan-500/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-2">Reusable Workflow Security Model</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Granular Least Privilege:</strong> You can explicitly map only <code class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">DEPLOY_KEY</code> without leaking <code class="text-rose-400 font-mono">STRIPE_SECRET</code> to child jobs.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">OIDC Federated Claims:</strong> The GitHub OIDC token generated inside a reusable workflow contains the <code class="text-cyan-300 font-mono">job_workflow_ref</code> claim, enabling AWS IAM and GCP Workload Identity to enforce that cloud infrastructure can only be modified by specific blessed workflows.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Runner Isolation:</strong> Even if code in an earlier test job is compromised via dependency confusion, it cannot read memory or disk artifacts from subsequent reusable deployment jobs.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-emerald-500/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Composite Action Security Boundaries</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">No Direct Secret Access:</strong> Composite actions cannot evaluate <code class="text-amber-300 font-mono">&#36;&#123;&#123; secrets.TOKEN &#125;&#125;</code> directly, protecting them from accidentally leaking caller repository credentials.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Shared Process Memory:</strong> Because composite actions run in the same shell context, any preceding malicious build script or hijacked test suite can inspect environment variables or disk files left by the action.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Input Masking:</strong> When secrets are passed into a composite action as inputs (<code class="text-emerald-300 font-mono">with: token: &#36;&#123;&#123; secrets.API_KEY &#125;&#125;</code>), GitHub Actions automatically masks the string in console logs, but child processes must be careful not to write values unescaped.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 7: Nesting Limits & Anti-Patterns -->
    <section class="mb-12 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">07.</span> Nesting Limits, Call Stack Depth &amp; Anti-Patterns
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        Both paradigms impose architectural constraints to prevent infinite loops and runner starvation. Understanding these hard platform limits prevents breaking pipeline failures during critical production releases:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-[#0F172A] my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-xs sm:text-sm text-left border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/70 text-slate-400 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase">
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Modularity Construct</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Hard Call-Stack Limit</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Common Anti-Pattern</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4">Architectural Remedy</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/60 text-slate-300 text-xs">
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Reusable Workflows</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-cyan-300 font-bold">4 Levels Max</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-300">Over-nesting (Repo &rarr; Org &rarr; Base &rarr; Deploy &rarr; Notify triggers immediate parser abort).</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">Flatten the hierarchy into modular sibling jobs using <code class="font-mono">needs:</code> dependencies.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Composite Actions</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold">10 Levels Max</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-300">Wrapper explosion (creating a composite action that only calls another composite action with 1 line of bash).</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">Consolidate tightly coupled steps into cohesive domain-specific actions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Action &harr; Workflow Cycles</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-rose-400 font-bold">Prohibited</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-300">Attempting to call a reusable workflow from inside a composite action step.</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">Strictly prohibited by GitHub schema; composite actions can only call other actions.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 8: Architectural Decision Guide -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">08.</span> Architectural Decision Flowchart: When to Use Which?
      </h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-cyan-500/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-2">Select Reusable Workflows When:</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Enterprise Compliance Gates:</strong> You need an un-bypassable security scanning and production deployment gate centrally mandated across 50+ repositories.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Multi-OS / Multi-Version Matrix:</strong> You need to spawn 12 matrix jobs across Ubuntu, Windows, and macOS from a centralized pipeline specification.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Isolated Environment Secrets:</strong> You want production cloud credentials restricted strictly to deployment jobs without exposing them to preceding test jobs.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-emerald-500/40">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Select Composite Actions When:</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Repetitive Step Sequences:</strong> You find yourself copying the same 5-10 setup steps (Node/Rust install, cache restore, credential helper) across 8 different workflow jobs.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Wall-Clock Latency Minimization:</strong> You cannot tolerate the 30-second virtual machine provisioning latency required to spin up an external job.</li>
            <li>&bull; <strong class="text-white">Shared Workspace Mutations:</strong> You need the action to modify files, write config, and install dependencies directly in the caller job's working directory.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

